Ladybird’s tabs.query() needs no permission, and returns a tab’s url and
title only with the tabs permission or a host permission matching the
URL, so a url or title filter never matches a tab whose details the
caller can’t see. Chromium does the same: TabsQueryFunction::Run()
filters with MatchesTab(), which consults
ExtensionTabUtil::GetScrubTabBehavior() before comparing url or title.
Gecko’s TabBase.hasTabPermission() gates the same properties on the tabs
permission, an activeTab grant, or a host permission, and its query()
skips url and title matches the extension can’t see. WebKit’s
WebExtensionContext::tabsQuery() also hides url and title without
access, but can ask the user for access mid-query; Ladybird doesn’t
prompt, as Chromium and Gecko don’t.

The schema errors (an unexpected property, a wrong type, an enum value
outside its list) and the bad-pattern error ("Invalid url pattern
'<pattern>'") are worded exactly as Chromium’s api_invocation_errors.cc
and url_pattern_set.cc word them, since extensions see and compare them.
Chromium’s filters that Ladybird can’t answer yet (audible, muted,
autoDiscardable, frozen, splitViewId) reject with Ladybird’s own message
rather than matching every tab. The tab object follows Chromium’s
ExtensionTabUtil::CreateTabObject(): selected and highlighted follow
active, and groupId is -1.
